In article <1992Aug5.212010.6060@usenet.ins.cwru.edu>,
lake@cwjcc.INS.CWRU.Edu (Dr. Robin Lake) writes:
|>
|>We have a world-wide VAXNotes capability, which I use daily.  I also have
|>been a user of 'rn' for the UNIX news for a decade or so.
|>
|>IMHO, VAXNotes is seriously brain-damaged.  One can NOT follow a "thread"
|>through a VAXNotes Conference (no CTRL-n equivalent).

VAX NOTES conferences are by their very nature organized by "threads".  They
are called "topics" in NOTES, and the fundamental organization of NOTES is a
two-level hierarchy of topics (threads) with a series of sequential replies
(articles within the topic).  The keypad "1" and "3" keys move backwards and
forwards through the sequence of replies to a particular topic or thread.
The 1, 2, 3, and 5 keys parallel the "inverted-T" configuration of the arrow
keys:  5 and 2 are the "up" and "down" keys and move through the topics.  1 and
3 are the "left" and "right" keys and move through the replies associated
with a topic.

If you wish to look at the notes in the way that one normally views threads in
a newsgroup, that is, go to the first new posting on a thread and then look
through all the other articles on that thread, you can use the sequence
of keypad-comma key (the NEXT UNSEEN command, which moves you to the next
unseen note, chronologically, in a topic other than the current one), followed
by keypad 3 (the NEXT REPLY command, which moves you to the next reply in a
topic).  You can do "one key NOTEing" using the ENTER key, which does a NEXT
UNSEEN followed by NEXT REPLY until the topic is exhausted, then NEXT UNSEENs
to the next topic with unread notes.

If you are using the DECwindows interface to NOTES, you can use Linkworks
to establish Hypertext links between notes in a conference, or between notes
and mail messages, calendar entries, etc.

|>  VAXNotes require
|>active administration if ageing of notes is desired/required (interested
|>in a Note on a seminar held two years ago?).

You are assuming that you WANT the ageing of notes.  VAX NOTES was designed to
provide permanent archives of discussions.  For example, we commonly use NOTES
around here to carry on design discussions.  It's often quite useful several
years later, when doing maintenance on a piece of code, to go back and read
the discussion so that you find out why things were implemented the way they
were.

Yes, a PURGE command or some such thing to age notes would be a worthy
one-plus to the current product.

|>  There is no way to search
|>by title ---  one must suffer the delay of searching the entire texts in
|>a Topic or Conference.

Check out DIRECTORY/TITLE.

|>  There are no "regular expression" searches.

True.  Maybe in a future version.

|>I often thought that DEC built VAXNotes so that an organization's DECnet
|>traffic would be increased, requiring more hardware to sustain the network!
|>:=}}}

Darn.  He saw through it!  :-)

|>I have not RTFM for VAXNotes, just used the on-line help.  A lot of
|>non-intuitive commands needed for the occasional administration of things.

Maybe you should.  You might learn something.

|>Just an opinion  ---   and worth what you paid for it!

Indeed.

I think that calling something "brain-damaged" just because it doesn't have
quite the same user interface or set of features that you're used to is being
overly harsh.

I don't usually respond to flames of this sort, but I wanted to correct the
factual errors in the posting.

--PSW


