A new product which supports information sharing across and
within groups is the result of research at the University of New
South Wales.  It is now commercially available in the US, Europe,
SE Asia, and Australia.

Of particular interest may be the ability to scan Usenet bulletin
boards, index items automatically using your own terminology,
selectively distribute items according to individuals' interest
profiles, and support commenting internally on the significance
of items.  The readership list is automatically augmented as a
discussion increases in importance, or new topics are added.

An abbreviated description is given below.  Email me for more
details of the product, its availability or the research that let
to its unique specification on brookes@cumulus.csd.unsw.oz.au.
Alternatively call Dave Wilkie on (404) 977 3126 in the US.


                grapeVINE - Innovative Groupware


grapeVINE is able to index, prioritise, selectively disseminate,
and store for retrieval a wide variety of information in text or
document form.  In addition, it supports commenting and other
forms of added-value items such as ideas, opinions and rumours.
A multi-threaded database, or corporate memory, is created;
linking related items.

Users maintain their own interest profiles, using keywords drawn
from a thesaurus that is also the basis of the indexing
procedure.  Item importance is also used as a parameter for
profile building and selective distribution.

The information items that can be automatically "seeded" into
grapeVINE for classification and distribution include news,
reports, graphics, spreadsheets, CD-ROM documents, reports and
forecasts.  The comments and other items originating direct from
professionals using the system may include assessments,
suggestions and problems.  Delivery, and commenting, escalation,
etc. can be by workstation or electronic mail.

Application of these concepts can be valuable in many executive,
professional and decision support environments including market
intelligence, technology and current affairs awareness, problem
management and project coordination.  Automatic (ie undirected)
mail forwarding is also valuable in large networks.

grapeVINE operates on VAX mainframes with vt100/200/300
terminals, with or without ALL-IN-1, or as a client server system
on Unix, OS/2, VAX servers, with Windows, Macs, OS/2 clients
across standard networks.  The email interface allows remote or
unskilled users to have access.

A multi-server capability selectively transfers items across
large networks, and supports cross server discussions.


grapeVINE - The key to informed organizations


