In article <1992Aug5.212010.6060@usenet.ins.cwru.edu> lake@po.CWRU.Edu writes:
[...]
>
>IMHO, VAXNotes is seriously brain-damaged.  One can NOT follow a "thread"
>through a VAXNotes Conference (no CTRL-n equivalent).  VAXNotes require
>active administration if ageing of notes is desired/required (interested
>in a Note on a seminar held two years ago?).  There is no way to search
>by title ---  one must suffer the delay of searching the entire texts in
>a Topic or Conference.  There are no "regular expression" searches.
[...]
>I have not RTFM for VAXNotes, just used the on-line help.  A lot of
>non-intuitive commands needed for the occasional administration of things.

 Are we talking about the same VAXnotes product ?

 There most certainly *IS* a way to follow threads.  Pressing KP3 takes
you to next reply in a particular topic (i.e. 100.0 to 100.1); pressing
KP1 moves you back to the previous reply in that topic.

 Searching by title is done with the command, DIR/TITLE="some string".
This provides a listing of all the main topics that match the string;
using DIR/ALL/TITLE="some string" gives you a listing of not only the
main topics, but also of any replies whose titles match the string.
Contrary to your statement, it does *NOT* search the body of the note
for the string.  If note-writers have attached keywords to their replies, 
it's also possible to search for notes whose keywords match.

 You are correct in your statement that VAXnotes doesn't support
searches based on regular expressions.

