  I must be an old-timer. I remember when DEC folks were helpful and tried
to correct misconceptions about DEC products. I guess now it's everone for
himself and try to be the first one out the door.

  Instead of making some non-productive snide comment, how about looking in
the US Software Price List (current edition 29-Jun-1992, EC-C0824-98)? On
pages 679 and 680 you'll find the pricing for Notes, which ranges from $2180
on a 10-unit system (such as a VAXstation) through $70524 for a 9000-unit
system (which is equivalent to almost two VAX 9000-440's). Those prices are
before discount, BTW.

  However, that isn't the most efficient way to go. You could either purchase
concurrent user licenses for the number of users you expect to use Notes at
any given time, personal use license for designated users, or (most cost-
effective) a full-function license for the node that holds the Notes confer-
ences and client licenses for the other nodes. Assuming a 4000-300 as the
server (and that's a _big_ server) at $2180, you can get the same 9000 units
of client for $13208. To compare that with the $30000 of the original quote
(which I assume was actually a 2400-unit license at $31160), you'd use a 2400-
unit client license at $5850 with the 10-unit server license for a total of
$8030, again before discount.

